            <ArticleTitle>Revisiting Oxycodone Analgesia: A Review and Hypothesis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e163-e174</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1932-2275(17)30027-7</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.anclin.2017.01.022</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Oxycodone, a semisynthetic opioid analgesic, is widely used in clinical practice. Oxycodone and morphine seem to be equally effective and equipotent; however, morphine is 10 times more potent than oxycodone when given epidurally. This article provides an updated review of the basic pharmacology of oxycodone with a special focus on pharmacokinetic/pharmacodynamics properties. The controversy regarding oxycodone-mediated effects for visceral pain via agonism and the possible role of peripheral opioid analgesia are discussed in the present investigation in an attempt to propose a plausible explanation to the perplexing question of oxycodone analgesia.</AbstractText>
